    {
      "path": "pro.finalArgument.thesis",
      "text": "A specifically Christian explanation gains credibility when independent theistic considerations raise its prior plausibility and resurrection evidence then supplies historical discrimination.",
      "openingTenWords": "A specifically Christian explanation gains credibility when independent theistic considerations"
    },
    {
      "path": "pro.finalArgument.conclusion",
      "text": "The affirmative case is strongest as a disciplined comparative inference whose Christian conclusion remains proportional to the reliability and independence of its historical evidence.",
      "openingTenWords": "The affirmative case is strongest as a disciplined comparative inference"
    },
    {
      "path": "pro.newArguments.0",
      "text": "Stable convergence among reflective moral judgments could favor a reality in which rational agents track objective value, especially if the convergence persists after correcting for power, parochial custom, and immediate advantage. A divine-nature account would gain support if it predicts both common moral recognition and principled criticism of inherited commands better than evolutionary debunking or simple convention. The test must be comparative: disagreement and moral error count against an easy providential prediction, while reform under shared reasons may count in its favor.",
      "openingTenWords": "Stable convergence among reflective moral judgments could favor a reality"
    },
    {
      "path": "pro.newArguments.1",
      "text": "The rapid adoption of resurrection belief may carry additional evidential weight when witnesses accepted substantial social costs without obvious material advantage. Sincerity cannot establish truth, and later reports of persecution must be assessed carefully, but costly commitment can narrow explanations based on deliberate fabrication. Combined with early testimony, it creates a focused comparison among sincere visionary experience, memory development, communal reinforcement, and encounter with a risen Jesus rather than a crude choice between fraud and miracle.",
      "openingTenWords": "The rapid adoption of resurrection belief may carry additional evidential"
    },
    {
      "path": "con.finalArgument.thesis",
      "text": "Rational nonbelief is warranted when the Christian hypothesis lacks distinctive predictions and its key evidence remains compatible with more familiar, independently supported processes.",
      "openingTenWords": "Rational nonbelief is warranted when the Christian hypothesis lacks distinctive"
    },
    {
      "path": "con.finalArgument.conclusion",
      "text": "Until Christianity predicts and explains the disputed evidence more distinctly than its rivals, withholding belief is proportionate even without proving that every divine reality is impossible.",
      "openingTenWords": "Until Christianity predicts and explains the disputed evidence more distinctly"
    },
    {
      "path": "con.newArguments.0",
      "text": "A perfectly loving God seeking relationship with every capable person appears to predict widespread, accessible, and noncoercive evidence sufficient for sincere seekers. Persistent reasonable nonbelief would therefore be evidence against at least that conjunction of attributes, provided the inquiry distinguishes resistance, cultural isolation, cognitive limitation, and ordinary uncertainty. This argument improves on generic absence claims because it identifies a particular expectation generated by the Christian hypothesis and specifies the observation whose prevalence would lower that hypothesis’s probability.",
      "openingTenWords": "A perfectly loving God seeking relationship with every capable person"
    },
    {
      "path": "con.newArguments.1",
      "text": "Miracle testimony should be evaluated through a common framework covering source proximity, independence, public accessibility, failed predictions, transmission history, and available natural explanations. Applying the same framework across Christian and non-Christian claims prevents tradition-specific discounting while allowing evidential differences to matter. If several incompatible traditions score similarly, confidence in any unique revelation should fall; if Christianity clearly performs better, the con must acknowledge that discriminator rather than treating all supernatural reports as interchangeable mythology.",
      "openingTenWords": "Miracle testimony should be evaluated through a common framework covering"
    }
  ],
